  "body": "<p>The UAE is one of the few markets in the region where a business can build a substantial public record in English. It is also a market where readers, partners and editors notice quickly whether you actually operate here.</p>\n<p>This page explains how the press works, who it suits, and what a realistic route in looks like.</p>\n<h2 id=\"who-press-here-suits\">Who press in the UAE suits, and who it does not</h2>\n<p>Gulf coverage works best for businesses with something real on the ground: an office, customers, staff, a launch or an investment in the region. Founders relocating to Dubai or Abu Dhabi, regional executives, luxury and hospitality brands, property and finance firms, and experts who can explain a market to local readers all have a natural place in this press.</p>\n<p>It suits people less well when the only connection is aspiration. A company with no presence, customers or plans in the region will struggle to interest an editor here, however strong its story is elsewhere. In that case a UAE dateline is not the right first target, and we will say so.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-the-gulf-media-market-works\">How the Gulf media market works</h2>\n<p>Three broad groups of publication matter to most English-language campaigns, and they behave very differently.</p>\n<h3>English-language dailies</h3>\n<p>Long-running newspapers such as <a href=\"/get-featured-in/gulf-news\">Gulf News</a> and Khaleej Times cover national news, business, property, technology and daily life for a broad readership of Emiratis, expatriates and people following the country from abroad. They are staff newsrooms. Coverage is earned, and a genuine UAE angle decides almost everything.</p>\n<h3>Regional business titles</h3>\n<p>Business publications such as Arabian Business cover companies, markets, property and the people running them across the Gulf. They look for consequence: expansion, investment, jobs, sector data and executives who can explain what is changing.</p>\n<h3>Licensed luxury and lifestyle editions</h3>\n<p>Esquire Middle East, Harper's Bazaar Arabia and Grazia Middle East are licensed regional editions of international brands, produced locally with their own editorial teams. They are editorially separate from their parent editions abroad, so a feature in one is regional-edition coverage and should be described that way. They cover fashion, beauty, luxury, culture and society, and they expect strong visuals and careful timing.</p>\n<p>Alongside these sit smaller digital publications such as Dubai Weekly, which publish founders and businesses a national daily would not have space for. They are useful, and they are not national mastheads.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-environment-you-are-pitching-into\">The environment you are pitching into</h2>\n<p>Media in the UAE operates within local content and advertising rules, and commercial material is expected to be clearly labelled. In practice that means advertising, sponsored content and paid features should be identifiable as such, and editorial should never be presented as something it is not.</p>\n<p>Much of the region's media conversation also happens in Arabic. English-language coverage reaches a large and influential readership, but it is one part of the picture rather than all of it. If your audience is primarily Arabic-speaking, plan for that from the start.</p>\n<p>Local context matters too. A pitch that reads as careless about where it is being sent will not get far. None of this is an obstacle to good PR. It is simply the standard a professional campaign should meet anyway.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-realistic-route-in\">What a realistic route in looks like</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Earned editorial.</strong> A journalist decides your story is worth covering, profiles you, or quotes you as an expert. Free, competitive and never guaranteed.</li><li><strong>Expert commentary.</strong> Being a reliable, quotable voice on stories already running. Often the most durable way into the dailies and business titles.</li><li><strong>Sponsored and advertising content.</strong> Paid, produced commercially and labelled on the page. Legitimate, and not the same as being written about.</li><li><strong>Managed features on smaller digital titles.</strong> A team writes a dedicated piece and handles placement. Faster and more predictable, and it should be described accurately afterwards.</li></ul>\n<p>The right mix depends on where you are starting. Many founders begin with a well-written permanent feature and regional business coverage, then build toward the dailies and licensed magazines once there is a record for editors to check. Our guide to <a href=\"/get-featured-in/arabian-business\">getting featured in Arabian Business</a> covers the business route in more detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-works-with-dna\">How it works with DNA</h2>\n<p>You share your story: what you do, what has changed, and who you want to reach in the region. We develop the angle an editor here would actually recognise, write it to editorial standard and identify the titles where the fit is real.</p>\n<p>You approve every word before anything is submitted. We tell you plainly which route each opportunity is, earned or paid, and we do not promise an editor's decision on your behalf. Publication at a staff-written newsroom is always the newsroom's call.</p>\n<p>If the UAE is not the right market for your story yet, we will say that too. When you are ready, <a href=\"/contact\">get in touch</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Do I need a UAE office to get press here?</h3>\n<p>You need a genuine UAE angle. An office helps, but customers, a launch, an investment or regional expertise can also give an editor a reason to care. With no connection at all, the pitch is a hard sell.</p>\n<h3>Is Harper's Bazaar Arabia the same as Harper's Bazaar?</h3>\n<p>No. It is a licensed regional edition with its own editors, separate from the US and UK editions. The same principle applies to Esquire Middle East and Grazia Middle East.</p>\n<h3>Can you guarantee coverage in Gulf News or Khaleej Times?</h3>\n<p>No. Editorial coverage at a staff-written daily is decided by its newsroom. Advertising and sponsored content are separate, labelled products, and anyone guaranteeing earned editorial is describing something they do not control.</p>",
