  "body": "<p>British media is crowded, fast and sceptical. Journalists here receive far more pitches than they can read, and they are quick to spot a company announcement dressed up as a story.</p>\n<p>That makes the UK hard, and it makes coverage here worth having. This page covers who it suits, how the market is structured and what a realistic route in looks like.</p>\n<h2 id=\"who-uk-press-suits\">Who UK press suits, and who it does not</h2>\n<p>UK coverage suits businesses and experts with a British audience to reach: customers, investors, hires or partners in the UK. It suits founders with original data, a defensible argument or a consumer angle that affects readers directly. It suits musicians, labels, and fashion and beauty brands with a real presence in the British market.</p>\n<p>It suits people less well when the story has no British dimension. A UK national desk is not interested in a launch that only affects another country, and a licensed British edition wants British relevance just as much. If your buyers are elsewhere, spend the effort where they read.</p>\n<h2 id=\"national-press-and-licensed-editions\">National press and licensed UK editions</h2>\n<p>The distinction that matters most in the UK is between national newsrooms and licensed British editions of international titles.</p>\n<h3>National newsrooms</h3>\n<p>National titles such as <a href=\"/get-featured-in/independent\">The Independent</a> have their own newsrooms covering news, politics, business, culture and lifestyle. Journalism is written by staff or commissioned by editors. There is no open contributor programme to join, so every route ends with a journalist deciding you are worth the space.</p>\n<p>News desks and feature desks work very differently. News moves by the hour and wants something happening. Lifestyle, culture, travel and wellbeing desks run to a slower rhythm and can be more open to contributed expertise.</p>\n<h3>Licensed British editions</h3>\n<p>Rolling Stone UK and Elle UK are licensed British editions of international brands, with their own editors commissioning for a UK readership. They are editorially separate from the US titles. Coverage in Elle UK is not coverage in Elle US, and pitching one does not reach the other.</p>\n<p>That distinction matters when you describe coverage afterwards. Name the edition that published the piece. Investors, partners and journalists check, and precision protects the credibility the coverage was meant to build.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-the-uk-market-differs\">How the UK market differs</h2>\n<p>A few things set British media apart from other English-language markets.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Competition is intense.</strong> National journalists are pitched constantly, so short, specific and evidenced pitches are the only kind that get read.</li><li><strong>Scepticism is the default.</strong> Superlatives, unsupported claims and marketing language count against you. A checkable number counts for you.</li><li><strong>Commercial content is labelled.</strong> Advertising and sponsored features are expected to be identifiable as such, and editorial and paid content are treated as different things.</li><li><strong>Specialist and trade press runs deep.</strong> Many sectors have their own titles, and for B2B businesses they are often a better first target than a national masthead.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"a-realistic-route-in\">What a realistic route in looks like</h2>\n<p>For the national press, the honest route is earned. Be a source rather than a subject: journalists need credible people to quote, often at short notice, and being useful and fast is how relationships begin. Bring something checkable, such as a dataset, a survey or a document. Pitch the editor who owns your subject, not a general address.</p>\n<p>For licensed culture and fashion editions, timing and assets matter as much as the story. A release, a collection, a tour or a campaign needs to reach the right editor early, with images ready.</p>\n<p>Paid routes exist in the UK as everywhere: advertising, sponsored content and managed features on smaller digital titles. They are legitimate when they are labelled and described accurately. They are not a journalist choosing to write about you, and nobody should present them that way. Our guide to <a href=\"/get-featured-in/independent\">getting featured in The Independent</a> explains the earned route at a national title in more depth.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-works-with-dna\">How it works with DNA</h2>\n<p>You share your story and the audience you want to reach in Britain. We find the angle a UK editor would take seriously, write it to editorial standard and pitch the desks and editions where the fit is real.</p>\n<p>You approve every word before anything is submitted. We tell you which route each opportunity is, and we never guarantee a national placement, because nobody outside a newsroom can. When a national title is not realistic yet, we will say so and build the record that makes it realistic later. You can see the wider range of titles we work with on our <a href=\"/publications\">publications page</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Can I pay to be featured in The Independent?</h3>\n<p>Not for editorial coverage. Advertising and sponsored content are separate commercial products and are labelled. Editorial is the newsroom's decision.</p>\n<h3>Is Rolling Stone UK the same as Rolling Stone?</h3>\n<p>No. It is a licensed British edition with its own editorial team, separate from the US magazine. Describe coverage there as Rolling Stone UK.</p>\n<h3>Should a US company pitch UK media?</h3>\n<p>Only with a genuine British angle: UK customers, a launch, hires, investment or data about the UK market. Without one, the pitch is unlikely to land, however strong the story is at home.</p>",
