  "body": "<p>Australian media is smaller than many founders expect, and more connected. There are fewer mastheads, fewer journalists on each beat, and reputations, good and bad, travel quickly between them.</p>\n<p>This page explains who press in Australia suits, how the market is structured, and what a realistic route in looks like.</p>\n<h2 id=\"who-australian-press-suits\">Who Australian press suits, and who it does not</h2>\n<p>It suits Australian founders, small and medium businesses, and established companies with a local story to tell. It suits international businesses doing something that genuinely matters here: opening an office, hiring, raising from local investors or serving Australian customers. It suits artists, screen businesses, health experts and fashion brands with a real presence in the Australian market.</p>\n<p>It suits people less well when the Australian connection is thin. Local editors write for local readers. A global announcement with an Australian city added to the first line reads exactly like what it is, and in a small market the same editors will remember it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-the-australian-market-differs\">How the Australian media market differs</h2>\n<h3>A small, concentrated market</h3>\n<p>With fewer titles to go around, each placement carries more weight, and each poor pitch costs more. Journalists move between publications and often know each other's work, so a reputation for sending useful, accurate material is an asset in its own right.</p>\n<p>It also changes how you pitch. Choose the title where the fit is strongest and pitch it properly, rather than sending the same email to every desk at once. In a market this size, a mass pitch is easy to spot, and it rarely leaves a good impression.</p>\n<h3>Licensed Australian editions</h3>\n<p>Several international brands publish here under licence. <a href=\"/get-featured-in/forbes-australia\">Forbes Australia</a>, Rolling Stone Australia, Variety Australia, Men's Health Australia and Harper's Bazaar Australia are produced locally with their own editorial teams. They are editorially separate from their US and UK parents.</p>\n<p>That has two consequences. Pitching the Australian edition does not reach the American one, and coverage should be described by its correct name. A feature in Forbes Australia is Forbes Australia coverage, and presenting it as anything else is misleading.</p>\n<p>Precision helps with editors too. A pitch that shows you know which edition you are writing to, and what it has published recently, is read very differently from one addressed to the brand in general.</p>\n<h3>Local newsrooms and business titles</h3>\n<p>Alongside the licensed editions sit Australian newsrooms: established dailies such as The Canberra Times, and business titles such as SmartCompany, written for people running small and medium businesses. These are staff-written, and coverage is earned. There are also smaller digital titles such as Australian Times, written for Australians abroad, where managed features are a common route.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-realistic-route-in\">What a realistic route in looks like</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Earned editorial.</strong> A journalist decides your story is worth covering. Free, competitive and never guaranteed.</li><li><strong>Expert commentary.</strong> Being quick, credible and quotable when a reporter needs a voice on a story already running.</li><li><strong>Trade press.</strong> For B2B businesses, sector titles often reach buyers more directly than a general masthead.</li><li><strong>Sponsored content and managed features.</strong> Paid, labelled and legitimate when described accurately. Not the same as being written about.</li></ul>\n<p>Most businesses are best served by building in order: a well-written permanent feature or trade coverage first, then local business titles, then the licensed editions and dailies once there is a record editors can check.</p>\n<p>Our guide to <a href=\"/guides/how-to-get-press-in-australia\">getting press in Australia</a> walks through that sequence. There are separate guides on <a href=\"/guides/pr-for-australian-small-business\">PR for Australian small businesses</a> and <a href=\"/guides/australian-trade-publications-for-b2b\">Australian trade publications for B2B brands</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-works-with-dna\">How it works with DNA</h2>\n<p>You share your story and who you need to reach in Australia. We develop the angle a local editor would recognise, write it to editorial standard and pitch the titles where the fit is real.</p>\n<p>You approve every word before anything is submitted. We tell you which route each opportunity is, earned or paid, and we do not promise an editor's decision. Publication at a staff-written newsroom is always the newsroom's call.</p>\n<p>If a licensed masthead is not realistic yet, we will say so and start where coverage will actually build. When you are ready, <a href=\"/contact\">get in touch</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Is Forbes Australia the same as Forbes?</h3>\n<p>No. It is a licensed Australian edition with its own editorial team, editorially separate from Forbes US. The same principle applies to the other licensed Australian editions.</p>\n<h3>Do I need to be based in Australia?</h3>\n<p>You need an Australian story. International companies are covered when they are doing something here that matters to Australian readers, such as hiring, launching or serving local customers.</p>\n<h3>Can you guarantee coverage in an Australian newspaper?</h3>\n<p>No. Editorial coverage in a staff-written newsroom is decided by that newsroom. Sponsored content is a separate, labelled product, and anyone guaranteeing earned editorial is promising something they do not control.</p>",
