  "body": "<p>Canadian media is often treated as an extension of the American market. It is not. It has its own national conversation, strong city papers, a bilingual audience and its own editors deciding what matters to Canadian readers.</p>\n<p>This page explains who press in Canada suits, how the market works and what a realistic route in looks like.</p>\n<h2 id=\"who-canadian-press-suits\">Who Canadian press suits, and who it does not</h2>\n<p>It suits Canadian founders and companies with a story that matters nationally or in a specific city. It suits businesses with financial or economic significance in Canada, experts who can explain a policy, market or housing question, and consumer brands with a real presence in Toronto, Montreal, Vancouver or elsewhere. It suits fashion and beauty brands selling to Canadian customers.</p>\n<p>It suits people less well when there is no Canadian dimension. A US launch with no Canadian customers, staff or plans will not interest a Canadian desk, however large it is at home.</p>\n<p>It also suits people less well when the goal is instant visibility. The major Canadian newspapers are staff-written, so coverage takes a genuine story, time and a reporter's interest. If you need guaranteed placement on a set date, that is advertising, and it should be bought and described as advertising.</p>\n<h2 id=\"national-versus-city-papers\">National titles versus city papers</h2>\n<p>The first decision in any Canadian campaign is level.</p>\n<h3>National titles</h3>\n<p>The <a href=\"/get-featured-in/financial-post\">Financial Post</a> covers business, markets and the economy for readers across the country, and the National Post covers national news, politics, business and opinion. Both look for national relevance. A story that only concerns one city usually belongs with a city paper, unless it illustrates something happening across Canada.</p>\n<h3>City papers</h3>\n<p>The Toronto Sun, Montreal Gazette and Vancouver Sun serve their cities and regions. Local relevance is the strongest hook a city paper responds to, and a founder with a genuine local story competes in a far smaller field than the same founder pitching a national desk.</p>\n<p>These newspapers are staff-written. There is no contributor programme to join and no fee that turns into a news article.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-the-canadian-market-differs\">How the Canadian market differs</h2>\n<h3>A bilingual market</h3>\n<p>Canada works in English and French, and Quebec is where that matters most. Much of the public conversation in the province happens in French-language media. English-language coverage in the Montreal Gazette reaches an important readership, but if your audience is Quebec as a whole, plan for French-language media as well.</p>\n<h3>Shared content across titles</h3>\n<p>Several major Canadian dailies, including the Financial Post, National Post, Toronto Sun, Montreal Gazette and Vancouver Sun, operate within a shared newspaper network where content can run across titles. A story written for one paper can appear in others. That can extend a story's reach, but it does not remove the need for a local angle. Our guide on <a href=\"/guides/why-canadian-newspapers-share-content\">why Canadian newspapers share content</a> explains what that means for a pitch.</p>\n<h3>Licensed editions</h3>\n<p>Elle Canada is the licensed Canadian edition of the international Elle brand, editorially separate from other editions. Coverage there is Elle Canada coverage and should be described that way.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-realistic-route-in\">What a realistic route in looks like</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Earned editorial.</strong> A reporter decides your story is worth covering. Free, competitive and never guaranteed.</li><li><strong>Expert commentary.</strong> Business and city reporters need informed voices on stories already moving, often the same day.</li><li><strong>Local first.</strong> A strong city story is often more realistic than a national one, and it builds the record national editors check.</li><li><strong>Advertising and sponsored content.</strong> Paid and labelled. Legitimate, and not a journalist choosing to write about you.</li></ul>\n<p>Our guides to <a href=\"/guides/how-to-get-press-in-canada\">getting press in Canada</a> and <a href=\"/guides/pr-for-canadian-small-business\">PR for Canadian small businesses</a> go into each route in more detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-works-with-dna\">How it works with DNA</h2>\n<p>You share your story and where in Canada your audience is. We decide with you whether it is a national or a local story, develop the angle, write it to editorial standard and pitch the desks where the fit is real.</p>\n<p>You approve every word before anything is submitted. We do not sell placements in staff-written Canadian newspapers, and we will tell you honestly whether a national title is realistic now or a goal to build toward.</p>\n<p>If it is not realistic yet, we will start with the city paper where your story is strongest and build from there. When you are ready, <a href=\"/contact\">get in touch</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Should I pitch a national paper or a city paper?</h3>\n<p>It depends on the story. National titles want significance across Canada. City papers want a concrete local connection. Most businesses find a city story more realistic to start with.</p>\n<h3>Do I need French-language coverage?</h3>\n<p>If your audience includes Quebec as a whole, it is worth planning for. English-language coverage reaches part of that audience, not all of it.</p>\n<h3>Can you guarantee a placement in the National Post?</h3>\n<p>No. Editorial coverage at a staff-written national paper is the newsroom's decision. Anyone guaranteeing it is describing something they do not control.</p>",
