  "h1": "PR Agency in Nigeria",
  "title": "PR Agency in Nigeria: Get Featured in Leading Media",
  "description": "A PR agency for founders, brands and artists in Nigeria: we shape your story and place it in Business Insider Africa, Pulse, Pulse Sports, Time and Billboard.",
  "intro": "DNA works with founders, brands, artists and executives in Nigeria to turn real stories into features in Nigerian, pan-African and international titles, with your approval on every word.",
  "outlets": [],
  "body": "<p>A PR agency in Nigeria helps you get your company, product or personal story in front of the readers who decide your reputation, from customers in Lagos and Abuja to investors and partners abroad. For start-ups, established firms and creative talent, that means coverage in titles people already recognise and trust.</p><p>We work with businesses in Nigeria from the first idea to the published feature. We develop the story, write it to the publication's standard, manage the placement, and you approve every word before it goes live. You get coverage you can share with clients and investors, and that shows up when people search your name.</p><h2 id=\"who-pr-in-nigeria-suits\">Who PR in Nigeria suits</h2><p>PR in Nigeria suits companies and people ready for a wider audience. That includes Lagos fintech and technology start-ups preparing to raise funds, consumer brands growing across West Africa, fashion and beauty labels, real estate and hospitality businesses, and energy and industrial firms. It also suits musicians, film makers, athletes and creatives building a profile at home and internationally, and executives taking on regional or global roles.</p><h2 id=\"the-nigeria-media-landscape\">The Nigeria media landscape</h2><p>Nigeria has a large, competitive and outspoken press. Well-known national papers such as The Punch, The Guardian, Vanguard, ThisDay and BusinessDay cover politics, business and society, and digital titles reach a young audience that reads mainly on mobile.</p><p>We place stories in Business Insider Africa, which covers start-ups, funding, markets and business leaders across the continent, and in Pulse, which reaches younger Nigerian readers through news, entertainment and lifestyle. Pulse Sports suits footballers, athletes, clubs and sports businesses. For international reach, Time carries weight with global readers, while Billboard is a natural home for Nigerian artists, labels and music businesses. Each title has a distinct audience, so the story is shaped for each one rather than sent everywhere unchanged.</p><h2 id=\"what-stories-travel-in-nigeria\">What stories travel in Nigeria</h2><p>The strongest Nigerian stories connect to what the country is known for. Lagos has become a major start-up centre, and fintech in particular, from payments to digital banking, gives founders strong angles on growth, funding and expansion. Afrobeats has carried Nigerian music around the world, and Nollywood makes the country a major force in film, so artists, producers and entertainment businesses have stories with real international interest.</p><p>Oil and gas remain central to the economy, and companies in energy, power and infrastructure have plenty to say about investment and new projects. Agriculture and food processing, real estate in Lagos and Abuja, and a lively fashion scene all produce stories that readers follow. Football is a national passion, which gives sports businesses and athletes a ready audience.</p><ul><li><strong>Founder and funding stories</strong> for business readers across Africa</li><li><strong>Music, film and culture stories</strong> for Nigerian and international audiences</li><li><strong>Sport stories</strong> for fans of football and athletics</li></ul><h2 id=\"local-national-and-international-coverage-together\">Local, national and international coverage together</h2><p>Nigerian businesses often sell across West Africa and raise money from investors in Europe and North America, and Nigerian artists have fans across the diaspora. A feature in a Nigerian title builds trust at home, coverage in a pan-African title reaches investors and partners across the continent, and an international title helps when you are speaking to collaborators, buyers or audiences further afield.</p><p>We plan placements so they support each other rather than repeat the same article. A funding story in a business title and a personal profile in an entertainment or lifestyle title give different readers two reasons to remember you.</p><h2 id=\"how-it-works-with-dna\">How it works with DNA</h2><p>We start with a conversation about your business, your audience and what you want the coverage to do. We then recommend the titles that fit and the angle for each. Next we develop the story, write it to the publication's standard, manage the placement, and you approve every word before it goes live.</p><p>We check names, job titles and company details with you before anything is finalised. Timelines depend on each title, and most placements take weeks rather than days.</p><h2 id=\"publications-we-place-in-nigeria\">Publications we place in across Nigeria</h2>\n<p>A selection of the titles we place clients in. Where a title has its own page, it explains what it publishes and how a feature there works.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Business Insider Africa</strong></li><li><strong>Pulse.ng</strong></li><li><strong>Pulse Sports</strong></li><li><strong>Time.com</strong></li><li><strong>Billboard</strong></li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Do I need a PR agency based in Nigeria?</h3><p>No. What matters is knowing which titles your audience reads and how to write for them. We work with businesses in Nigeria remotely and handle the full process, from the angle to the approved feature.</p><h3>How do I get featured in Nigerian newspapers and media?</h3><p>Start with a story that fits the title, such as a funding round, a product launch, a new release or a clear view on your industry. We shape the angle, write the piece to the publication's standard and manage the placement.</p><h3>What does a PR agency in Nigeria do?</h3><p>It finds the story in your business or career, matches it to the right Nigerian, African and international publications and turns it into coverage. With DNA that means planning, writing, placement and your approval of every word.</p>",
  "toc": [{"id": "who-pr-in-nigeria-suits", "text": "Who PR in Nigeria suits"}, {"id": "the-nigeria-media-landscape", "text": "The Nigeria media landscape"}, {"id": "what-stories-travel-in-nigeria", "text": "What stories travel in Nigeria"}, {"id": "local-national-and-international-coverage-together", "text": "Local, national and international coverage together"}, {"id": "how-it-works-with-dna", "text": "How it works with DNA"}, {"id": "publications-we-place-in-nigeria", "text": "Publications we place in across Nigeria"}, {"id": "frequently-asked-questions", "text": "Frequently asked questions"}],
  "takeaways": ["A PR agency in Nigeria places your story in titles such as Business Insider Africa, Pulse, Pulse Sports, Time and Billboard.", "Strong Nigerian stories tie to fintech, Afrobeats and Nollywood, energy, real estate, fashion and football.", "DNA develops the story, writes it to each publication's standard and manages the placement, and you approve every word before it goes live."],
  "kind": "country",
